speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I rise on a matter of urgent and pressing necessity under the provisions of Standing Order 43. In view of the fact that import quotas to protect footwear manufacturers in eastern Canada are causing cowboy boot retailers in Edmonton to sing the blues, since only one Canadian manufacturer of cowboy boots exists and it cannot meet the volume or the speciality demands of the western consumer, and in view of the fact that any proper cowboy boot store must have at least 300 styles of boots, as does Mr. Porayko in Edmonton, ranging in price
from $65 to more than $600 and including 40 lines of boots crafted in ostrich, crocodile, alligator and other exotic leathers, 1 move, seconded by the hon. member for Capilano (Mr. Huntington):
That this House direct the Minister of Industry, Trade and Commerce to pull on his boots, pick up his lariat and corral the rest of the cabinet to a round-up where the problems of cowboy boot sellers and consumers will be recognized and the import restrictions loosened before the said minister and his colleagues are loosened from office by the western electorate.

That in the opinion of this House it is crucial to the strength of Canada's economy and the provision of employment opportunities that a vigorous program of technological research and development be promoted by the government, and to this end this House urges a policy which would:
(1)	set a goal of 2.5 per cent of the G.N.P. to be spent on research and development by 1985;
(2)	significantly increase the research and development tax credit for Canadian industry;
(3)	give particular priority to strengthening our research and development programs, particularly in the fields of agriculture and renewable resources;
(4)	extend the tax credit system to provide incentive for new employment in industrial research, design and development;
(5)	encourage development work in areas of acknowledged Canadian expertise.

ALLOTTED DAY S.O. 58-RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT POLICY The House resumed consideration of the motion of Mr. Clark:
That in the opinion of this House it is crucial to the strength of Canada's economy and the provision of employment opportunities that a vigorous program of technological research and development be promoted by the government, and to this end this House urges a policy which would:
(1)	set a goal of 2.5 p. cent of the G.N.P. to be spent on research and development by 1985;
(2)	significantly increase the research and development tax credit for Canadian industry;
(3)	give particular priority to strengthening our research and development programs, particularly in the fields of agriculture and renewable resources;
(4)	extend the tax credit system to provide incentive for new employment in industrial research, design and development;
(5)	encourage development work in areas of acknowledged Canadian expertise.
